{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01T6conLopLkvQmxxv2pECuE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JpZHczgndVxXzBYLfoRRdQ","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus is **novelty and related work**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\"Understanding and Calibrating Benchmark Contamination in LLMs\" presents CONTAMCAL, a two-stage framework: (1) a signal pipeline computing per-item exposure scores via 4 detection signals aggregated through RRF, and (2) a contamination-augmented 4PL IRT model estimating per-benchmark γ_k (contamination susceptibility) via Bayesian MCMC, producing calibrated scores with credible intervals.\n\nThree claimed contributions:\n- C1: Signal characterization across 4 signals, 3 scales (0.5B–3B), 5 dosages. Only S1 (embedding retrieval) works (AUC>0.95). Min-K++ exhibits directional reversal (Cohen's d = −1.2 to −2.7).\n- C2: Fisher Information identifiability bounds: structural (AUC≥0.55), practical (AUC≥0.70, N≥50). Validated via 120-condition simulation sweep.\n- C3: Proof-of-concept calibration: 4.2 pp MAE on strong-effect benchmarks, 85% CI coverage at 95% nominal, competitive with 7 baselines including DCR.\n\n## Related Work as Presented in Paper (Section 2)\n- **Contamination Detection**: Min-K++ (Shi et al., 2024), ConStat (Yang et al., 2023), Self-Critique (Golchin and Surdeanu, 2024). Paper claims these detect but don't calibrate.\n- **Contamination Mitigation**: Option shuffling, rephrasing (Li and Flanigan, 2024), dynamic benchmarks (White et al., 2024). Zhang et al. (2025) showed 20 strategies fail systematically.\n- **IRT for LLM Evaluation**: Lalor et al. (2024), Growing Pains/Polo et al. (2024), HiBayES. None model contamination as covariate.\n- **Calibration Disambiguation**: \"Calibration\" here = recovering true scores (not probability calibration à la Guo et al., 2017 / Platt, 1999). Conformal prediction (Angelopoulos and Bates, 2023) noted but dismissed due to exchangeability violations.\n- Key theoretical lineage: Explanatory IRT (De Boeck and Wilson, 2004), item compromise modeling (Eckerly, 2017; Sinharay, 2017).\n\n## Key Paper Details\n- 4PL+γ model: P(Y_ij=1 | θ_i, γ_k) = c_j + (d_j − c_j)σ(a_j(θ_i − b_j + γ_k · e_ij))\n- Calibrated score: θ̂_cal = argmax P(θ | Y, γ_k=0)\n- Fisher Information for γ_k derived in Appendix A\n- RRF used for signal aggregation (training-free, avoids meta-circularity)\n- Stan NUTS sampler, per-benchmark fitting\n- Results: Only 1/4 signals effective (S1 embedding). RRF degrades vs S1-only. Calibration matches Emperor mitigation (MAE 4.2) but adds UQ. γ mostly not significant (only GSM8K CI excludes zero).\n\n## Key Experimental Scale\n- Primary: Qwen2.5 0.5B/1.5B/3B, N=26 models\n- 7B bridging: 10 runs\n- RL observational: 8 models, degraded signals\n- Cross-family: Llama-3.2-1B, 6 runs\n- Simulation: 120 conditions, 1200 runs\n- Benchmarks: MMLU, ARC-C, GSM8K, HumanEval\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focused on:\n1. **Novelty assessment**: How novel is the 4PL+γ IRT formulation? Is the connection to item compromise modeling (Eckerly, Sinharay) sufficiently differentiated? Is the Fisher Information identifiability analysis a genuine theoretical contribution or a straightforward application?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Are there missing references? Is the positioning against DCR (Oren et al., 2024) fair? What about other IRT-for-LLM work? Any missing contamination detection/mitigation methods?\n3. **Contribution significance**: Given that (a) only 1/4 signals works, (b) RRF degrades vs single signal, (c) calibration improvement is 0.7pp over best baseline, (d) γ mostly doesn't exclude zero — are the contributions sufficient for a top venue?\n4. **Framing honesty**: The paper reframes from \"calibration framework\" to \"signal characterization + identifiability + proof-of-concept.\" Is this reframing convincing or does it feel like post-hoc rationalization of weak results?\n5. **Broader impact and practical utility**: Who would use this system? Is there a clear use case given the limitations?\n\nFormat your review as:\n### Summary\n### Strengths (bulleted)\n### Weaknesses (bulleted, numbered W1, W2, ...)\n### Questions for Authors (numbered Q1, Q2, ...)\n### Minor Issues\n### Score (1-6) and Confidence (1-5)\n","model":"opus"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4129,"cache_read_input_tokens":61069,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4129},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"65318bdd-9a4d-466f-be88-bd9a1ec29eb0","uuid":"87b1bcb7-68bf-47c0-b244-9a37f6757622"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a60157210cadba6f2","tool_use_id":"toolu_01JpZHczgndVxXzBYLfoRRdQ","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus is **novelty and related work**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\"Understanding and Calibrating Benchmark Contamination in LLMs\" presents CONTAMCAL, a two-stage framework: (1) a signal pipeline computing per-item exposure scores via 4 detection signals aggregated through RRF, and (2) a contamination-augmented 4PL IRT model estimating per-benchmark γ_k (contamination susceptibility) via Bayesian MCMC, producing calibrated scores with credible intervals.\n\nThree claimed contributions:\n- C1: Signal characterization across 4 signals, 3 scales (0.5B–3B), 5 dosages. Only S1 (embedding retrieval) works (AUC>0.95). Min-K++ exhibits directional reversal (Cohen's d = −1.2 to −2.7).\n- C2: Fisher Information identifiability bounds: structural (AUC≥0.55), practical (AUC≥0.70, N≥50). Validated via 120-condition simulation sweep.\n- C3: Proof-of-concept calibration: 4.2 pp MAE on strong-effect benchmarks, 85% CI coverage at 95% nominal, competitive with 7 baselines including DCR.\n\n## Related Work as Presented in Paper (Section 2)\n- **Contamination Detection**: Min-K++ (Shi et al., 2024), ConStat (Yang et al., 2023), Self-Critique (Golchin and Surdeanu, 2024). Paper claims these detect but don't calibrate.\n- **Contamination Mitigation**: Option shuffling, rephrasing (Li and Flanigan, 2024), dynamic benchmarks (White et al., 2024). Zhang et al. (2025) showed 20 strategies fail systematically.\n- **IRT for LLM Evaluation**: Lalor et al. (2024), Growing Pains/Polo et al. (2024), HiBayES. None model contamination as covariate.\n- **Calibration Disambiguation**: \"Calibration\" here = recovering true scores (not probability calibration à la Guo et al., 2017 / Platt, 1999). Conformal prediction (Angelopoulos and Bates, 2023) noted but dismissed due to exchangeability violations.\n- Key theoretical lineage: Explanatory IRT (De Boeck and Wilson, 2004), item compromise modeling (Eckerly, 2017; Sinharay, 2017).\n\n## Key Paper Details\n- 4PL+γ model: P(Y_ij=1 | θ_i, γ_k) = c_j + (d_j − c_j)σ(a_j(θ_i − b_j + γ_k · e_ij))\n- Calibrated score: θ̂_cal = argmax P(θ | Y, γ_k=0)\n- Fisher Information for γ_k derived in Appendix A\n- RRF used for signal aggregation (training-free, avoids meta-circularity)\n- Stan NUTS sampler, per-benchmark fitting\n- Results: Only 1/4 signals effective (S1 embedding). RRF degrades vs S1-only. Calibration matches Emperor mitigation (MAE 4.2) but adds UQ. γ mostly not significant (only GSM8K CI excludes zero).\n\n## Key Experimental Scale\n- Primary: Qwen2.5 0.5B/1.5B/3B, N=26 models\n- 7B bridging: 10 runs\n- RL observational: 8 models, degraded signals\n- Cross-family: Llama-3.2-1B, 6 runs\n- Simulation: 120 conditions, 1200 runs\n- Benchmarks: MMLU, ARC-C, GSM8K, HumanEval\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focused on:\n1. **Novelty assessment**: How novel is the 4PL+γ IRT formulation? Is the connection to item compromise modeling (Eckerly, Sinharay) sufficiently differentiated? Is the Fisher Information identifiability analysis a genuine theoretical contribution or a straightforward application?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Are there missing references? Is the positioning against DCR (Oren et al., 2024) fair? What about other IRT-for-LLM work? Any missing contamination detection/mitigation methods?\n3. **Contribution significance**: Given that (a) only 1/4 signals works, (b) RRF degrades vs single signal, (c) calibration improvement is 0.7pp over best baseline, (d) γ mostly doesn't exclude zero — are the contributions sufficient for a top venue?\n4. **Framing honesty**: The paper reframes from \"calibration framework\" to \"signal characterization + identifiability + proof-of-concept.\" Is this reframing convincing or does it feel like post-hoc rationalization of weak results?\n5. **Broader impact and practical utility**: Who would use this system? Is there a clear use case given the limitations?\n\nFormat your review as:\n### Summary\n### Strengths (bulleted)\n### Weaknesses (bulleted, numbered W1, W2, ...)\n### Questions for Authors (numbered Q1, Q2, ...)\n### Minor Issues\n### Score (1-6) and Confidence (1-5)\n","uuid":"76d10cd2-de33-49fb-afe7-1f83641d9168","session_id":"65318bdd-9a4d-466f-be88-bd9a1ec29eb0"}
