{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EEqimA9ZMyS1mNrQPAiqfp","type":"tool_result","content":"100\t\\label{sec:method}\n101\t\n102\t\\subsection{Overview}\n103\t\n104\t\\contamcal{} operates in two stages (Figure~\\ref{fig:pipeline}).\n105\t\\textbf{Stage~1} computes per-item exposure scores for each model via four detection signals aggregated through Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF).\n106\t\\textbf{Stage~2} fits a contamination-augmented 4PL IRT model using these exposure scores as covariates, estimating per-benchmark contamination susceptibility $\\gamma_k$ and producing calibrated scores with Bayesian credible intervals.\n107\t\n108\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n109\t\\centering\n110\t\\fbox{\\parbox{0.95\\columnwidth}{\\small\n111\t\\textbf{Stage 1: Signal Pipeline} \\\\\n112\t$\\text{S1: Embedding retrieval} \\to \\text{per-item similarity}$ \\\\\n113\t$\\text{S2: Min-K\\%++} \\to \\text{per-item MIA score}$ \\\\\n114\t$\\text{S3: ConStat} \\to \\text{original vs.\\ paraphrase gap}$ \\\\\n115\t$\\text{S4: Self-Critique} \\to \\text{entropy-based detection}$ \\\\\n116\t$\\xrightarrow{\\text{RRF}}$ Composite exposure score $e_{ij}$ \\\\[4pt]\n117\t\\textbf{Stage 2: 4PL+$\\gamma$ IRT} \\\\\n118\t$P(Y_{ij}=1) = c_j + (d_j - c_j)\\,\\sigma\\bigl(a_j(\\theta_i - b_j + \\gamma_k \\cdot e_{ij})\\bigr)$ \\\\\n119\t$\\xrightarrow{\\text{Stan NUTS}}$ Calibrated score $\\hat{\\theta}_i$ with CI\n120\t}}\n121\t\\caption{The \\contamcal{} pipeline.}\n122\t\\label{fig:pipeline}\n123\t\\end{figure}\n124\t\n125\t\\subsection{Detection Signals}\n126\t\n127\tWe employ four detection signals spanning different information sources:\n128\t\n129\t\\textbf{S1---Embedding Retrieval.} A pre-trained sentence transformer computes cosine similarity between training data and benchmark items.\n130\tThis signal is model-agnostic: it does not depend on the target model's parameters, yielding stable AUC across model scales (\\S\\ref{sec:signal_char}).\n131\t\n132\t\\textbf{S2---Min-K\\%++} \\citep{shi2024detecting}. Computes the $k$-th percentile of token log-probabilities, under the assumption that memorized text has elevated low-probability token scores.\n133\tWe find this assumption fails under SFT contamination (\\S\\ref{sec:signal_char}).\n134\t\n135\t\\textbf{S3---ConStat} \\citep{yang2023rethinking}. Measures the performance gap between original and paraphrased items. Contaminated items show systematically higher accuracy on originals.\n136\t\n137\t\\textbf{S4---Self-Critique.} Measures entropy differences between original and paraphrased items, testing whether the model exhibits lower uncertainty on memorized content.\n138\t\n139\t\\subsection{Reciprocal Rank Fusion}\n140\t\n141\tGiven $K$ signals, each producing a ranking $r_k(i,j)$ over items $j$ for model $i$, the composite exposure score is:\n142\t\\begin{equation}\n143\te_{ij} = \\sum_{k=1}^{K} \\frac{1}{c + r_k(i,j)}\n144\t\\label{eq:rrf}\n145\t\\end{equation}\n146\twhere $c=60$ is the standard RRF constant \\citep{cormack2009reciprocal}.\n147\tRRF is training-free, avoiding the meta-circularity of learned aggregation: a learned weighting function trained on contamination labels may itself be contaminated.\n148\t\n149\t\\subsection{Contamination-Augmented 4PL IRT}\n150\t\\label{sec:irt_model}\n151\t\n152\tWe extend the four-parameter logistic (4PL) IRT model with a contamination covariate.\n153\tFor model $i$ responding to item $j$ on benchmark $k$:\n154\t\\begin{equation}\n155\tP(Y_{ij} = 1 \\mid \\theta_i, \\gamma_k) = c_j + (d_j - c_j)\\,\\sigma\\!\\bigl(a_j(\\theta_i - b_j + \\gamma_k \\cdot e_{ij})\\bigr)\n156\t\\label{eq:4pl_gamma}\n157\t\\end{equation}\n158\twhere $\\theta_i$ is model ability, $a_j$ is item discrimination, $b_j$ is item difficulty, $c_j$ is the guessing parameter, $d_j$ is the upper asymptote, $\\gamma_k$ is the per-benchmark contamination susceptibility, and $e_{ij}$ is the composite exposure score.\n159\t\n160\tThe \\emph{calibrated score} is obtained by setting $\\gamma_k = 0$:\n161\t\\begin{equation}\n162\t\\hat{\\theta}_i^{\\text{cal}} = \\arg\\max_\\theta P(\\theta \\mid \\mathbf{Y}_i, \\gamma_k = 0)\n163\t\\end{equation}\n164\t\n165\tWe fit the model using Stan's NUTS sampler with weakly informative priors.\n166\tPer-benchmark fitting (rather than joint across benchmarks) reduces local independence violations inherent in single-model IRT \\citep{mislevy1986recent}.\n167\t\n168\t\\paragraph{Measurement Error Extension.}\n169\tThe simple model treats $e_{ij}$ as known.\n170\tOur measurement error (ME) extension models exposure as a noisy observation of latent true exposure $\\tau_{ij}$:\n171\t$e_{ij} = \\tau_{ij} + \\epsilon_{ij}$, $\\epsilon_{ij} \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, \\sigma_e^2)$.\n172\tThis propagates detection uncertainty into calibrated score posteriors.\n173\t\n174\t\\subsection{Fisher Information Identifiability Analysis}\n175\t\\label{sec:fisher}\n176\t\n177\tWe derive the Fisher Information for $\\gamma_k$ in the 4PL+$\\gamma$ model to establish identifiability conditions.\n178\tThe key insight is that $I(\\gamma_k)$ depends on the quality of exposure scores (signal AUC) and sample size ($N_\\text{models}$).\n179\t\n180\t\\textbf{Structural identifiability} (AUC$\\geq$0.55): $\\gamma$ is recoverable in principle---$I(\\gamma) > 0$ and the parameter is not confounded with $\\theta$.\n181\t\n182\t\\textbf{Practical identifiability} (AUC$\\geq$0.70, $N\\geq$50): $\\gamma$ is recoverable with useful precision---RMSE$<$0.04, CI coverage$>$90\\%.\n183\t\n184\tThe theoretical Fisher Information matches empirical estimates with ratio 0.998 (\\S\\ref{sec:identifiability}).\n185\tMeasurement error attenuates information: at noise level $\\sigma_e$, effective information scales as $I_{\\text{eff}} \\approx I(\\gamma) \\cdot (1 - \\sigma_e^2 / (\\sigma_e^2 + \\sigma_\\tau^2))$.\n186\t\n187\t%==============================================================================\n188\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n189\t\\label{sec:setup}\n190\t\n191\t\\paragraph{Controlled Contamination.}\n192\tWe inject benchmark items into SFT training data at five dosage levels: $d \\in \\{0\\%, 5\\%, 25\\%, 50\\%, 100\\%\\}$.\n193\tModels: Qwen2.5-\\{0.5B, 1.5B, 3B\\} with 2 random seeds per condition, yielding $N=26$ models pooled across scales.\n194\tAdditionally, Qwen2.5-7B with 5 dosage levels $\\times$ 2 seeds = 10 runs.\n195\t\n196\t\\paragraph{Benchmarks.}\n197\tMMLU (14K items, subsampled to 1K for NUTS), ARC-Challenge ($\\sim$1.2K items), GSM8K ($\\sim$1.3K items), and HumanEval (164 items, appendix only).\n198\t\n199\t\\paragraph{Baselines.}\n200\tSeven baselines: (0)~Raw scores, (1)~Remove-and-rescore, (2)~Single-signal 2PL+$\\gamma$, (3)~Vanilla 4PL ($\\gamma=0$), (4)~Linear regression + bootstrap CI, (5)~Emperor mitigation (rephrase + shuffle), (6)~DCR \\citep{oren2024proving}.\n201\t\n202\t\\paragraph{Evaluation Metrics.}\n203\tMAE (pp), 50\\%/80\\%/95\\% CI coverage, CI sharpness (width), Kendall's $\\tau$, dose-response monotonicity, and SBC diagnostics \\citep{talts2018validating}.\n204\t\n205\t%==============================================================================\n206\t\\section{Signal Characterization}\n207\t\\label{sec:signal_char}\n208\t\n209\t\\subsection{Signal $\\times$ Scale $\\times$ Benchmark AUC}\n210\t\n211\tTable~\\ref{tab:signal_auc} reports within-model item-level AUC at $d$005 (5\\% contamination) across three model scales and three benchmarks.\n212\t\n213\t\\begin{table}[t]\n214\t\\centering\n215\t\\small\n216\t\\caption{Detection signal AUC at $d$005 across scales and benchmarks. Only S1 (Embedding) achieves practical detection (AUC$>$0.70). S2--S4 remain near chance across all scales.}\n217\t\\label{tab:signal_auc}\n218\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}l ccc ccc ccc@{}}\n219\t\\toprule\n220\t& \\multicolumn{3}{c}{\\textbf{0.5B}} & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{\\textbf{1.5B}} & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{\\textbf{3B}} \\\\\n221\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-4} \\cmidrule(lr){5-7} \\cmidrule(lr){8-10}\n222\t\\textbf{Signal} & M & G & A & M & G & A & M & G & A \\\\\n223\t\\midrule\n224\tS1 Embed & .95 & .99 & \\textbf{1.0} & .96 & .98 & .99 & .96 & .97 & .99 \\\\\n225\tS2 MinK++ & .47 & .47 & .47 & .50 & .51 & .50 & .53 & .55 & .54 \\\\\n226\tS3 ConStat & .52 & .51 & .53 & .60 & .58 & .65 & .63 & .61 & .68 \\\\\n227\tS4 SelfCrit & .52 & .50 & .43 & .51 & .50 & .51 & .53 & .52 & .53 \\\\\n228\t\\bottomrule\n229\t\\end{tabular}\n230\t\\vspace{-2mm}\n231\t\\end{table}\n232\t\n233\tS1 (Embedding Retrieval) achieves AUC 0.953--0.995 across all conditions, because it is model-agnostic: a pre-trained sentence transformer computes training data similarity independently of the target model's parameters.\n234\t\n235\tS2 (Min-K++), S3 (ConStat), and S4 (Self-Critique) remain below the practical threshold of AUC$\\geq$0.70 across all three model scales.\n236\tS3 shows marginal improvement at larger scales (0.61--0.68 at 3B on ARC-C), but this is insufficient for reliable detection.\n237\tS2 and S4 hover near chance (0.47--0.55).\n238\tThe number of effective signals (AUC$>$0.70) is 1 at all scales.\n239\t\n240\tThis directly refutes a scale-dependent explanation: the failure of S2/S4 is \\emph{method-specific}, not scale-specific.\n241\tSFT changes the response distribution rather than input token probabilities, invalidating the low-probability-token assumption underlying Min-K++.\n242\t\n243\t\\subsection{Min-K++ Directional Reversal}\n244\t\\label{sec:reversal}\n245\t\n246\tAt high contamination ($d$100), Min-K++ does not merely fail---it \\emph{reverses direction}.\n247\tTable~\\ref{tab:cohens_d} reports cross-model Cohen's $d$ between $d$100 and $d$000 conditions.\n248\t\n249\t\\begin{table}[t]\n250\t\\centering\n251\t\\small\n252\t\\caption{Cross-model Cohen's $d$ ($d$100 vs.\\ $d$000). Negative $d$ for S2 indicates directional reversal: Min-K++ assigns \\emph{lower} anomaly scores to contaminated models.}\n253\t\\label{tab:cohens_d}\n254\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}l rrr rrr rrr@{}}\n255\t\\toprule\n256\t& \\multicolumn{3}{c}{\\textbf{0.5B}} & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{\\textbf{1.5B}} & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{\\textbf{3B}} \\\\\n257\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-4} \\cmidrule(lr){5-7} \\cmidrule(lr){8-10}\n258\t\\textbf{Signal} & M & G & A & M & G & A & M & G & A \\\\\n259\t\\midrule\n260\tS1 & 4.2 & 2.8 & 3.8 & 3.9 & 2.7 & 3.6 & 3.8 & 2.5 & 3.4 \\\\\n261\tS2 & $-$1.2 & $-$2.7 & $-$2.1 & $-$1.4 & $-$2.8 & $-$2.2 & $-$1.5 & $-$2.9 & $-$2.3 \\\\\n262\tS3 & 0.4 & 0.2 & 0.4 & 0.8 & 0.6 & 1.0 & 0.9 & 0.6 & 1.1 \\\\\n263\tS4 & 0.5 & 0.2 & 0.7 & 0.6 & 0.3 & 0.8 & 0.6 & 0.4 & 0.8 \\\\\n264\t\\bottomrule\n265\t\\end{tabular}\n266\t\\vspace{-2mm}\n267\t\\end{table}\n268\t\n269\tS2 shows large negative Cohen's $d$ ($-1.2$ to $-2.9$) consistently across all three scales and all three benchmarks.\n270\tWhen all items are contaminated ($d$100), the model's logit distribution shifts uniformly, causing Min-K++ to assign \\emph{lower} anomaly scores to contaminated models.\n271\tThis is not a labeling artifact---it persists at 1.5B ($d = -2.11$ mean) and 3B ($d = -2.24$ mean).\n272\tThe reversal represents a structural deficiency of membership inference approaches in the SFT contamination setting, independent of model scale.\n273\t\n274\tS1 maintains strong positive $d$ (2.5--4.2) across all conditions.\n275\tS3 and S4 show weak positive effects that grow modestly with scale but remain below practically useful magnitudes.\n276\t\n277\t\\subsection{Instruct Model Signal Degradation}\n278\t\n279\tOn Instruct-tuned models (RL post-training proxy), all signals degrade substantially.\n280\tS1 drops from AUC 0.97 (SFT) to 0.62 (1.5B-Instruct) and 0.58 (3B-Instruct).\n281\tS2 and S4 remain at chance (0.48--0.52).\n282\tRL post-training reshapes the logit distribution beyond what detection signals can track, limiting current applicability to the SFT regime.\n283\t\n284\t\\subsection{RRF Aggregation Ablation}\n285\t\\label{sec:rrf}\n286\t\n287\tTable~\\ref{tab:rrf} reports aggregation ablation results averaged across benchmarks at $d$005.\n288\t\n289\t\\begin{table}[t]\n290\t\\centering\n291\t\\small\n292\t\\caption{RRF aggregation ablation ($d$005, averaged across benchmarks).}\n293\t\\label{tab:rrf}\n294\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}l cc c@{}}\n295\t\\toprule\n296\t\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{AUC} & \\textbf{$\\pm$std} & \\textbf{vs.\\ S1} \\\\\n297\t\\midrule\n298\tS1 Embedding only & 0.979 & 0.018 & --- \\\\\n299\tRRF 4-signal ($k$=60) & 0.932 & 0.022 & $-$4.7\\pp{} \\\\"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01JpZHczgndVxXzBYLfoRRdQ","session_id":"65318bdd-9a4d-466f-be88-bd9a1ec29eb0","uuid":"8382fb9e-db20-4f62-8b7b-fa23d7f5e763","timestamp":"2026-05-14T20:02:26.230Z"}
